{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#2248","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#2248","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#2248\",\"parent_revision\":\"149814ce5f257589a758d537faaeaf509f42efb6\",\"root_revision\":\"9001ac3d542e97f36a9501f0fa0ad59158dbaa8a595e35aba728f0950e57f1a4\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P1\\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-12\\n-->\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer using Claude Code skills**, I want to **code-review: iron law structured diagnosis chain (symptom\u2192source\u2192consequence\u2192remedy) for every finding** so that **the tooling becomes more capable and complete**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Research entry: ./research/coding-agents/brooks-lint.md \u2014 pattern: Iron Law structured diagnosis chain\\n- **Priority**: P1\\n- **Added**: 2026-05-10\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Groomed (2026-05-17)\\n\\n### RT-ICA Assessment\\n\\n**Status: APPROVED-FOR-PLANNING**\\n\\n| Input | Status | Notes |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Functional intent | PRESENT | Replace flat finding format with 4-part Symptom/Source/Consequence/Remedy chain |\\n| Scope boundaries | PRESENT | `code-reviewer.md` Output Format section (lines 200\u2013211); 7 code-review-{stack} skills; `code-review-architecture` skill also present |\\n| External interfaces | PRESENT | brooks-lint Iron Law format with example in `research/coding-agents/brooks-lint.md` lines 178\u2013193 |\\n| Existing system context | PRESENT | Current format verified at `plugins/development-harness/agents/code-reviewer.md` lines 200\u2013211 |\\n| Constraints | DERIVABLE | Markdown must lint clean; format must be machine-parseable by the grep measurable-signal test |\\n| Verification expectations | PRESENT | Measurable signal section provides concrete grep command returning 4 distinct lines per finding |\\n\\nNo MISSING or BLOCKED conditions. Item is ready for planning.\\n\\n### Priority\\n\\n8/10 \u2014 The current flat format loses causal chain: a reviewer reading `[CORRECTNESS] src/foo.py:42 \u2014 wrong condition \u2014 fix it` has no indication of what principle flags this or what breaks downstream. The Iron Law format is directly cited in the brooks-lint benchmark as the structural change responsible for the 94% vs 16% accuracy gap. P1 classification is appropriate given the code-reviewer is a core S6 agent used in every feature workflow.\\n\\n### Impact\\n\\n- Blocks: #2253 (Health Score deductions depend on severity-weighted findings, which require structured finding objects to derive severity). #2251 (benchmark suite needs a stable finding schema to evaluate against).\\n- Unblocked by: #2250 (code-review-shared skill, which would house the canonical Iron Law definition; however #2248 can proceed without it since the format definition lives in the agent file itself).\\n- Bottleneck: Every code review output currently produced by the code-reviewer agent lacks the Source field, making it impossible to trace a finding back to a named principle or rule. The 7 stack skills list rules as flat bullets \u2014 there is no template enforcing structured expression.\\n\\n### Benefits\\n\\n- Code review findings become self-documenting: each finding names the principle and states the consequence without requiring the reviewer to have read the referenced book.\\n- Enables downstream parsing: the 4-line-per-finding structure makes programmatic extraction of severity (via Consequence) and automated fix suggestions (via Remedy) tractable.\\n- Enables the Health Score feature (#2253) which requires structured severity signals per finding.\\n- Aligns the code-reviewer agent with the brooks-lint benchmark methodology, enabling #2251 (eval suite) to validate against a known-good format.\\n\\n### Desired Structure\\n\\nAfter this item is complete, the following is observable:\\n\\n1. `plugins/development-harness/agents/code-reviewer.md` contains a section titled \\\"Iron Law\\\" or \\\"Four-Part Finding Format\\\" naming all four required fields explicitly.\\n2. The Output Format section (currently lines 200\u2013211) uses the four-part indented format:\\n   ```text\\n   - **[DIMENSION]** `{file}:{line}`\\n     - **Symptom**: {observed pattern}\\n     - **Source**: {principle name + rule reference}\\n     - **Consequence**: {regression surface, business impact, or maintainability risk}\\n     - **Remedy**: {concrete actionable fix}\\n   ```\\n3. Each `code-review-{stack}` skill rule entry includes a Source identifier (principle name or rule reference string) so the agent can cite it when producing a finding.\\n4. Running the measurable-signal grep against a known-bad sample produces exactly 4 distinct lines (`Symptom:`, `Source:`, `Consequence:`, `Remedy:`) per finding.\\n\\n### Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n1. `plugins/development-harness/agents/code-reviewer.md` Output Format section uses the 4-part indented format with `Symptom`, `Source`, `Consequence`, `Remedy` sub-bullets for every finding example.\\n2. The agent file contains a named section (\\\"Iron Law\\\", \\\"Four-Part Finding Format\\\", or equivalent) that defines all four fields and their semantics.\\n3. Each of the 7 (or 8 if `code-review-architecture` is included) `code-review-{stack}` SKILL.md files has at least one rule entry updated to include a Source identifier (principle or rule name).\\n4. Running `grep -E \\\"Symptom:|Source:|Consequence:|Remedy:\\\" {review_artifact_content}` against a review artifact produced by the updated agent returns exactly 4 lines per finding.\\n5. `uvx skilllint@latest check` passes on all modified SKILL.md files.\\n6. Existing universal quality dimensions (Security, Correctness, Tests, Contract, Naming, Error-Handling, Performance) are all preserved \u2014 no dimension is removed.\\n\\n### Resources\\n\\n| Type | Item |\\n|---|---|\\n| Prior work | `/home/user/claude_skills/plugins/development-harness/agents/code-reviewer.md` (lines 200\u2013211 \u2014 current Output Format) |\\n| Prior work | `/home/user/claude_skills/research/coding-agents/brooks-lint.md` (lines 178\u2013193 \u2014 Iron Law format with example) |\\n| Skill | `dh:code-review-python` (representative stack skill, flat rule format to be updated) |\\n| Skill | `dh:code-review-typescript`, `dh:code-review-nodejs`, `dh:code-review-web`, `dh:code-review-cli`, `dh:code-review-claude-skills`, `dh:code-review-llm`, `dh:code-review-architecture` |\\n| Skill | `plugin-creator:skill-creator` (skill authoring conventions) |\\n\\n### Dependencies\\n\\n- Depends on: None (can proceed independently; `code-review-shared` skill from #2250 is complementary but not prerequisite \u2014 the Iron Law definition can live in the agent file itself)\\n- Blocks: #2253 (Health Score needs structured findings with severity-derivable Consequence field); #2251 (eval suite needs stable finding schema to evaluate against)\\n- Related: #2250 (code-review-shared skill \u2014 shares the Iron Law concept; may supersede or absorb the Iron Law section added by this item if implemented after #2248); #2252 (auto-scope detection \u2014 independent, no ordering dependency)\\n\\n### Blockers\\n\\nNone. All required information is present:\\n- Current format verified from source file\\n- Target format specified with example from brooks-lint research\\n- Affected files enumerated (8 code-review-* skills + agent file)\\n- Measurable signal provides a concrete verification command\\n\\n### Effort\\n\\nSmall \u2014 The changes are confined to markdown content in one agent file and up to 8 skill SKILL.md files. No code, no schema changes, no MCP tool modifications. The format template is fully specified in the item. Risk: the 7 skill files each need at least one Source identifier added to existing rules; that is mechanical but repetitive across 8 files.\\n\\n### Fact Check\\n\\n**Finding format (current)** \u2014 Verified by reading `plugins/development-harness/agents/code-reviewer.md` lines 200\u2013211. The existing format is a single-line bullet with dimension tag, file:line, description, and fix.\\n\\n**brooks-lint Iron Law** \u2014 Verified against `research/coding-agents/brooks-lint.md` lines 178\u2013193. The four-part chain is: Symptom (observed pattern), Source (named principle + book), Consequence (regression surface / business impact), Remedy (concrete fix). Example from README.md lines 95\u201399 cited in research file.\\n\\n**94% vs 16% benchmark accuracy claim** \u2014 Cited in `research/coding-agents/brooks-lint.md` lines 51\u201352 and attributed to brooks-lint README.md lines 153\u2013166 (accessed 2026-05-10). Unverified against live source for this grooming pass.\\n\\n**Affected skill files** \u2014 8 code-review-* skills confirmed via Glob:\\n- `code-review-python`, `code-review-typescript`, `code-review-nodejs`, `code-review-web`, `code-review-cli`, `code-review-claude-skills`, `code-review-llm`, `code-review-architecture`\\n\\n`code-review-shared` skill does NOT yet exist (Glob returned no match) \u2014 tracked as #2250.\\n\\n## Target State\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n`plugins/development-harness/agents/code-reviewer.md` Output Format requires every finding to use the four-part structure:\\n\\n```text\\n- **[DIMENSION]** `{file}:{line}`\\n  - **Symptom**: {observed pattern}\\n  - **Source**: {principle name + rule reference, e.g., \\\"code-review-python \u2014 bare except is blocking\\\"}\\n  - **Consequence**: {regression surface, business impact, or maintainability risk}\\n  - **Remedy**: {concrete actionable fix}\\n```\\n\\nEach `code-review-{stack}` skill rule includes a Source identifier so the agent can cite it. The structured format matches brooks-lint's Iron Law (README.md lines 95\u201399, common.md lines 6\u201314) which achieves 100% structured findings vs. 0% for ungrounded LLM review (94% vs. 16% benchmark accuracy).\\n</div>\\n\\n## Measurable Signal\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nRun the code-reviewer agent against a known-bad sample. The artifact registered as `codebase-analysis` contains, for at least one finding:\\n\\n```bash\\ngrep -E \\\"Symptom:|Source:|Consequence:|Remedy:\\\" {artifact_content}\\n```\\n\\nreturns 4 distinct lines per finding. The agent prompt at `plugins/development-harness/agents/code-reviewer.md` contains a section titled \\\"Iron Law\\\" or \\\"Four-Part Finding Format\\\" that names all four required fields explicitly.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Current State\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n`plugins/development-harness/agents/code-reviewer.md` lines 200\u2013211 specify the Output Format for findings as:\\n\\n```text\\n- **[SECURITY|CORRECTNESS|TESTS|CONTRACT|NAMING|ERROR-HANDLING|PERFORMANCE]** `{file}:{line}` \u2014 {description} \u2014 {specific fix required}\\n```\\n\\nFindings include a dimension tag, a file:line reference, a description, and a fix. They do NOT include:\\n\\n- A \\\"Source\\\" field naming the principle, book, or rule that identifies the pattern\\n- A \\\"Consequence\\\" field stating why this matters (regression surface, business impact, maintenance risk)\\n- A consistent four-part structure that distinguishes the symptom from its causal source\\n\\nThe seven `code-review-{stack}` skills (e.g., `code-review-python/SKILL.md`) list rules as flat bullet points without the structured chain \u2014 there is no template requiring contributors to express each rule in Symptom/Source/Consequence/Remedy form.\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"df64521834b7fff0122c0868e9ade3943553913e7263db29710001f4512c5be2\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABPtXyKw\"}"}